#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "450aebfe-d896-50bc-a392-e43aee7afabd" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Rational Function With Two Linear Factors as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "28008aa6-6be6-52dd-9459-097113538b13" :question/math-academy-id "e-501" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} = \\dfrac{A}{(x+2)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x-3)}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above expression through by $(x+2)(x-3)$ gives\n\n$$\n2x-1= A(x-3)+B(x+2)\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2$ gets rid of the $B$ term and allows us to solve for $A$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(-2) - 1 &= A(-2 - 3) + B(-2 + 2) \\\\\n- 5 &= A(-5) + B(0) \\\\\nA &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3$ gets rid of the $A$ term and allows us to solve for $B$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(3) - 1 &= A(3 - 3) + B(3 + 2) \\\\\n5 &= A(0) + B(5) \\\\\nB &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKnowing that $A=1$ and $B=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} = \\dfrac{1}{(x+2)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x-3)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096410 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e66d7cf-1a4b-51e6-9c78-f70a8c0555df" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Rational Function With Three Linear Factors as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "d10d11a8-6a30-59b6-bb0e-6065e60bbc6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-502" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{2x^2+5x+7}{(x+2)(x-3)(x+1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$, $B$, and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+5x+7}{(x+2)(x-3)(x+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{x+2} + \\dfrac{B}{x-3} + \\dfrac{C}{x+1}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply both sides of the equation by $(x+2)(x-3)(x+1)$ to clear the fractions:\n\n$$\n2x^2+5x+7= A(x-3)(x+1)+B(x+2)(x+1)+C(x+2)(x-3)\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2$ gets rid of the $B$ and $C$ terms and allows us to solve for $A$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(-2)^{2} + 5(-2) + 7 &= A(-2 - 3)(-2 + 1) \\\\\n5 &= A(5) \\\\\nA &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3$ gets rid of the $A$ and $C$ terms and allows us to solve for $B$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(3)^{2} + 5(3) + 7 &= B(3 + 2)(3 + 1) \\\\\n40 &= B(20) \\\\\nB &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, plugging in $x=-1$ gets rid of the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(-1)^{2} + 5(-1) + 7 &= C(-1 + 2)(-1 - 3) \\\\\n4 &= C(-4) \\\\\nC &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKnowing that $A=1$, $B=2$, and $C=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+5x+7}{(x+2)(x-3)(x+1)} = \\dfrac{1}{x+2} +\\dfrac{2}{x-3} -\\dfrac{1}{x+1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "450aebfe-d896-50bc-a392-e43aee7afabd" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Rational Function With Two Linear Factors as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98c323c9-b4b7-55de-b8fb-b1fc59ed12ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255561} {:answer/id #uuid "3d30d3f4-7110-5d7a-94f1-05de257882c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255562} {:answer/id #uuid "db33c0c3-efd2-5101-8afc-0e00d60594ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255563} {:answer/id #uuid "3e25788b-7544-5f2b-9242-937076aff43b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255564} {:answer/id #uuid "c348aa84-f04d-5685-8e7d-055cf33fe3ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98c323c9-b4b7-55de-b8fb-b1fc59ed12ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255561} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc4fc8d6-5aac-5a3d-a9b2-82befae980c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca8ed292-7934-54b0-b0fb-7d888622b0ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-10974" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7x-37}{(5-3x)(2x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(5-3x)}+\\frac{B}{(2x+3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, calculate $A+B.$" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7x-37}{(5-3x)(2x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(5-3x)}+\\frac{B}{(2x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(5-3x)(2x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n7x-37=A(2x+3)+B(5-3x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-\\frac{3}{2})-37 & =A(2(-\\frac{3}{2})+3)+B(5-3(-\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\ \\frac{-21-74}{2} & =B(\\frac{10+9}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{-95}{2} & =B(\\frac{19}{2}) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-95}{19} \\\\ B & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{5}{3}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(\\frac{5}{3})-37 & =A(2(\\frac{5}{3})+3)+B(5-3(\\frac{5}{3})) \\\\ \\frac{35-111}{3} & =A(\\frac{10+9}{3}) \\\\ A & =\\frac{-76}{19} \\\\ A & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nA+B=-4+(-5)=-4-5=-9.\n$$"}]}
